---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageMeta from '@/components/PageMeta.astro';
import GatewayCard from '@/components/ia/GatewayCard.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Validation and examples"
  description="Understand the SHACL constraint layer, overlay profiles, gap register, diagnostic exemplars and validation evidence for the PDTF 1.0-derived ontology."
>
  <PageMeta category="PDTF 1.0 · extracted ontology" variant="brand" updated="Draft · under review" />
  <h1>Validation and examples</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    This category connects the ontology’s constraint layer to concrete diagnostic data. It
    explains what SHACL checks, how form-specific profiles constrain one shared class model,
    which source leaves remain recorded as gaps, and how exemplar graphs demonstrate expected
    conformance and failure behaviour.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--warn">
    <p class="callout__label">PDTF 1.0-derived draft</p>
    <p>
      Passing the documented checks shows conformance to tested rules in the draft corpus. It
      does not approve the semantic model, prove source data true, or establish an SPDTF 2.0
      standard. Known limits and validator requirements remain part of the result.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="layers">How the validation evidence fits together</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Base shapes</strong> express graph constraints separately from OWL class semantics.</li>
    <li><strong>Overlay profiles</strong> select form-specific constraints and record uncovered source leaves in the gap register.</li>
    <li><strong>Diagnostic exemplars</strong> pair instance graphs with expected reports for hard cases and negative tests.</li>
    <li><strong>Validation reports</strong> summarise generated-page and corpus checks without turning technical success into semantic approval.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="inspect">Inspect constraints and evidence</h2>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <GatewayCard href="/ontology/shapes" title="SHACL shapes" eyebrow="Constraint reference" description="Browse all 408 base node shapes, severity levels and SHACL-AF rules in the committed validation graph—the source of the historical rule that a form is its SHACL overlay." />
    <GatewayCard href="/ontology/profiles" title="Overlay profiles and gaps" eyebrow="Form-specific constraints" description="Inspect all 31 per-form profiles, most intentionally thin: they bind Category-G leaves and record the rest in the explicit gap register." />
    <GatewayCard href="/ontology/exemplars" title="Diagnostic exemplars" eyebrow="Worked data" description="Review all 17 worked instance graphs, each paired with an expected SHACL report, including conformant, non-conformant and hard-case examples." />
    <GatewayCard href="/model/validation-report" title="Model validation report" eyebrow="Generated assurance" description="See coverage and validation results for the generated model presentation and its underlying ontology corpus." />
  </div>

  <h2 id="interpret">Interpret results carefully</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">A green check can show…</th><th scope="col">It cannot show…</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>The input parsed and satisfied the tested SHACL configuration.</td><td>That every relevant business rule has been encoded.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A generated page agrees with the committed source used by its drift gate.</td><td>That the source definition has been approved by SPDTF 2.0 participants.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>An exemplar produced its recorded expected report.</td><td>That arbitrary real-world data or untested inference regimes behave identically.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Before relying on a result, review the <a href="/ontology/known-issues">known issues and validator caveats</a>
    and use the exact validation recipe on <a href="/ontology/usage#validation">Consume the ontology</a>.
  </p>
</Layout>
